  81: 
  82: ## 2. The fix â€” derive the type from the producer; delete the ability to declare it
  83: 
  84: **Decision D1. The suite stops naming its types. It names its requirement and its sut key, and the
  85: types are read off the adapter that actually supplies them.**
  86: 
  87: A new types-only module, `tests/at/harness/suite-adapters.ts`, holds the one declaration:
  88: 
  89: ```ts
  90: export type AdapterModules = {
  91:   'req-016': typeof import('../suites/req-016/_fixture.ts');
  92: };
  93: ```
  94: 
  95: and the derivations:
  96: 
  97: ```ts
  98: export type SuiteId = keyof AdapterModules;
  99: type AdapterOf<R extends SuiteId> = Awaited<ReturnType<AdapterModules[R]['createFixtureAdapter']>>;
 100: export type SutMapOf<R extends SuiteId> = AdapterOf<R>['sut'];
 101: export type SutKeyOf<R extends SuiteId> = keyof SutMapOf<R> & string;
 102: export type SutOf<R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>> = SutMapOf<R>[K];
 103: export type WorldOf<R extends SuiteId> = Awaited<ReturnType<AdapterOf<R>['fixtures']['world']>>;
 104: ```
 105: 
 106: `typeof import(...)` in a type position is erased at emit, so this adds **no runtime edge** from
 107: the harness to the suites. It mirrors, at compile time, the resolution
 108: `tests/at/harness/index.ts:32` already performs at run time.
 109: 
 110: `bindSuite` then becomes:
 111: 
 112: ```ts
 113: export function bindSuite<R extends SuiteId, K extends SutKeyOf<R>>(binding: {
 114:   requirement: R;
 115:   sut: K;
 116:   sutMissingDetail?: string;
 117: }): /* an atTest bound to SutOf<R, K> and WorldOf<R> */
 118: ```
 119: 
 120: **What this buys, concretely:**
 121: 
 122: 1. **There is no longer a type argument to lie with.** `bindSuite<NotificationsSut, World>` becomes
 123:    an arity error â€” the same defence, and the same diagnostic (TS2558), that killed the harness-type
 124:    attack in AI4DEV-24.
 125: 2. **The producer is the single source.** `_fixture.ts` already annotates `const sut: NotificationsSut`,
 126:    so the shape is checked where it is written. The consumer now *reads that* instead of restating it.
 127:    This is the brief's instruction â€” make the producer statically checked rather than asserting at the
 128:    consumer â€” satisfied without touching the factory.
 129: 3. **A mistyped sut key is a compile error.** `K extends SutKeyOf<R>` rejects `sut: 'notificatoins'`
 130:    where today it compiles and fails at run time as `sut-missing`.
 131: 4. **A required member the adapter does not supply now goes red at the adapter** (TS2741), because
 132:    there is only one type and the producer is annotated with it.
 133: 
 134: **Decision D2. `W` travels from the same adapter, but by its own path â€” it is not welded to `Sut`
 135: and it is never welded to the harness.**
 136: 
 137: `WorldOf<R>` derives from `fixtures.world`'s return type; `SutOf<R, K>` derives from `sut[K]`. Same
 138: source, separate derivations. The file warns twice that these were once conflated
 139: (`registry.ts:160-175`, `contracts.ts:151-165`) and AI4DEV-24 ruled on that boundary twice. To be
 140: unambiguous about which conflation is forbidden:
 141: 
 142: - **`h` is not re-parameterized. It stays concrete `AtHarness`.** Re-labelling the harness with the
 143:   suite's `W`/`Channel` is the door AI4DEV-24's Gate 1 and Gate 2 shut, and this item **must not
 144:   reopen it**. `OpenWorld.h` keeps its type and its comment.
 145: - `w` and `sut` are parameters of `OpenWorld` only, and they are now derived, not supplied.
 146: 
 147: For REQ-016 this makes `WorldOf<'req-016'>` resolve to the concrete `NotificationFixtureWorld`
 148: class rather than the `World` interface. That is *more* honest â€” it is what the producer really
 149: returns â€” and test bodies typed against `World` continue to work because the class implements it.
 150: 
 151: **Decision D3. The registry must be constrained so a world that cannot be torn down is rejected at
 152: the declaration**, i.e. the mapped type requires every entry's world to extend `WorldLike`. An
 153: adapter whose world forgets `teardown()` then fails in `suite-adapters.ts` where the entry is
 154: written, not in whichever suite first opens it.
 155: 
 156: **Decision D4. Close the last string-level gap with a runtime cross-check.**
 157: 
 158: Two strings must denote the same suite: the key used for the *type* lookup (`'req-016'`) and the
 159: path used for the *runtime* adapter load (`adapterUrl()` in `index.ts:32`, fed from the parsed AT
 160: id at `registry.ts:534`). They are currently independent.
 161: 
 162: `atTest` must therefore assert, at registration, that the AT id's requirement equals the bound
 163: requirement, and throw where it is written â€” the same doctrine the file already applies to a
 164: malformed id (`registry.ts:15`, `:44-49`). Once that holds, the type looked up and the module
 165: loaded are the same suite by construction, not by convention.
 166: 
 167: The message must name both values and the file, e.g.:
 168: `AT-017.03 was registered through a suite bound to req-016 â€” the harness would load req-016's
 169: fixture adapter while the type-check described req-017's`.
 170: 
 171: **Decision D4b. `bindSuite` is not the only door â€” the evidence-capture generics are a second one,
 172: and they must be closed in the same change.**
 173: 
 174: Found by re-reading before Gate 1 returned, and not by any reviewer.
 175: `tests/at/suites/req-016/d-taxonomy-evidence.test.ts:32` calls
 176: 
 177: ```ts
 178: defineEvidenceCapture<TaxonomyEvidence, NotificationsSut, World>('REQ-016 taxonomy execution', async ({ open }) => â€¦)
 179: ```
 180: 
 181: `defineEvidenceCapture` (`registry.ts:309`), `EvidenceCapture` (`:276`) and `AtContext` (`:193`) are
 182: each generic over `<Sut, W>`, and the producer receives a full `AtContext<Sut, W>` whose `open()`
 183: returns `OpenWorld<Sut, W>`. So a suite that never touches `bindSuite` can declare any SUT and world
 184: shape it likes and read them green â€” **the identical hole, through a door the design as first written
 185: did not mention.** Closing `bindSuite` alone would have moved the defect rather than removed it, and
 186: the item would have shipped claiming a closure it did not have.
 187: 
 188: So `bindSuite` must return a bound `defineEvidenceCapture` alongside the bound `atTest`, both
 189: carrying the derived `SutOf<R, K>` and `WorldOf<R>`; `_bind.ts` exports both; and
 190: `d-taxonomy-evidence.test.ts` imports the bound one instead of the raw generic. The raw generic must
 191: stop being reachable with suite-chosen type arguments â€” the same treatment `atTest` gets.
 192: 
 193: The general rule, which the executor applies rather than re-deriving: **every exported symbol in
 194: `registry.ts` that is generic over `Sut` or `W` is a door to this hole.** As of this tree that is
 195: `OpenWorld`, `AtContext`, `EvidenceCapture`, `defineEvidenceCapture`, `AtTestBody`,
 196: `executeRegisteredBody`, `InternalContext`, `atTest` and `bindSuite`. Each must either derive, or be
 197: unreachable from a suite with suite-supplied arguments.
 198: 
 199: **Decision D5. Extend the alias doctrine to the suite contract types that now flow through the seam.**
 200: 
 201: After D1, `OpenWorld.sut` resolves to `NotificationsSut` and `OpenWorld.w` to the adapter's world â€”
 202: both declared in `tests/at/suites/req-016/_contract.ts` as **`interface`**, along with everything
 203: their methods return (`SenderProbe`, `RegisteredRow`, `DocumentedDefault`, `NotificationEvent`,
 204: `Delivery`, `OpsItem`, `EmitResult`, `World`).
 205: 
 206: AI4DEV-24's rule (`contracts.ts:151-165`, `type-invention.selftest.ts:50-84`) is that everything
 207: reachable from the objects `open()` hands a test body must be a type alias, because an interface can
 208: be augmented and read green. These types are now exactly that. So they must become aliases and join
 209: the **exhaustive** `ALIAS_PROTECTED` list, which is explicitly a specification and not a sample.
 210: 
 211: I considered leaving this to a follow-up and **ruled against it**. Without it the item's own proof is
 212: nominal: it would close "a suite declares a different SUT type" and leave "a suite adds a member to
 213: the SUT type" open at the same seam, one line further down. Note the severity does drop either way â€”
 214: after D1 a *required* invented member breaks the producer with TS2741 â€” so what D5 actually kills is
 215: the *optional* member that reads `undefined`, which is precisely the case that slipped past two gates
 216: in AI4DEV-24 and is the reason the alias rule exists at all.
 217: 
 218: This is not scope drift: it is the same defect class, at the same seam, in the anchor the board item
 219: names. It is **not** H3/H4/H5 capability work and **not** a harness-factory redesign, both of which
 220: stay out.
 221: 
 222: ### D1 is not a hope â€” it was measured before briefing anyone
 223: 
 224: The single largest risk in this design was that `typeof import()` would not resolve the way Â§2
 225: claims, given a non-annotated returned object literal and a world class that is neither exported nor
 226: structurally plain (it has private members). That was settled empirically with a throwaway spike
 227: under the real `tests/at/tsconfig.json` and the pinned compiler, then deleted; transcripts are in
 228: `loop/items/AI4DEV-31/spike-raw.txt` and `spike-raw-variant.txt`.
 229: 
 230: | check | result |
 231: |---|---|
 232: | `SutOf<'req-016','notifications'>` assignable to `NotificationsSut` **and back** (exact match) | clean |
 233: | `WorldOf<'req-016'>` assignable to `World` | clean |
 234: | `WorldOf<'req-016'>` satisfies the `WorldLike` teardown constraint | clean |
 235: | a sut key the adapter does not supply | **TS2322**, and the message names the legal key set (`"notifications"`) |
 236: | reading a member `NotificationsSut` does not declare | **TS2339: Property â€¦ does not exist on type 'NotificationsSut'** |
 237: 
 238: The private members and the non-exported class caused no diagnostic and leaked no implementation
 239: name; TypeScript still displays the resolved type as `NotificationsSut` even with no local import of
 240: it. The mechanism works in this configuration.
 241: 
 242: ### Alternatives considered and rejected
 243: 
 244: | alternative | why rejected |
 245: |---|---|
 246: | Parameterize `AtHarness` / `createHarness` over the suite's `Sut` | This is the door AI4DEV-24 shut after two adversarial gates. Reopening it to close this hole would regress a fix that cost that item its Gate 1 and Gate 2. |
 247: | Runtime duck-type validation of the sut at `open()` (assert a method-name list) | The list cannot be derived from a type without a code generator, so it is a second hand-maintained declaration â€” the exact drift this item exists to remove. It would also make the proof a runtime test, where the brief requires a negative *type* test. |
 248: | Derive the requirement from the AT id at the type level with template-literal types | Requirement ids legitimately carry dots (`005.5` â€” `registry.ts:28-32`), so the parse is fragile, and it moves the derivation onto every call site instead of one place. |
 249: | Assert the shape at the consumer with a better-worded cast | It is still an assertion. The brief says prefer the checked producer, and the whole item is that assertions at this seam are the defect. |
 250: 
 251: ### The cost, stated plainly so it can be argued with
 252: 
 253: Every suite must have one line in `suite-adapters.ts`, and a new suite's `_bind.ts` will not compile
 254: until it is added. For thirty suites that is a thirty-line file. I judge that acceptable and in fact
 255: desirable â€” the harness's knowledge of which suites exist becomes explicit and checkable rather than
 256: a path convention â€” but it is a real ergonomic commitment beyond this item's own blast radius, so it
 257: is named here rather than buried. The compile error must therefore say exactly what to add.
 258: 
 259: ---
 260: 
 261: ## 3. The proof â€” a negative test, demonstrated RED first
 262: 
 263: The established pattern is `tests/at/typeprobes/` (a program that MUST NOT compile) plus
 264: `tests/at/harness/type-invention.selftest.ts` (which runs the compiler over it and fails **by name**
 265: if any attack is accepted). This item extends it.
 266: 
 267: **A NEW probe file, `tests/at/typeprobes/sut-seam.probe.ts`**, kept separate from
 268: `harness-invention.probe.ts` so the red-then-green demonstration can compile it in isolation.
 269: 
 270: Every attack must be written in **the form a suite can use on today's tree**, so the same file is
 271: meaningful before and after the fix. That is what makes the demonstration honest.
 272: 
 273: | # | attack | must be rejected by |
 274: |---|---|---|
 275: | 5 | fabricate the SUT type: `bindSuite<{ notThere(): Promise<void> }, World>({ sut: 'notifications' })`, then call `sut.notThere()` in a body | TS2558 (arity) |
 276: | 6 | fabricate the world type the same way and read a member off `w` | TS2558 (arity) |
 277: | 7 | name a sut key the adapter does not supply (`sut: 'notificatoins'`) | assignability error on the key |
 278: | 8 | read a member off `opened.sut` that the adapter's sut does not declare | TS2339 |
 279: | 9 | read a member off `opened.w` that the adapter's world does not supply | TS2339 |
 280: | 10 | merge a member into `NotificationsSut` via `declare module` | TS2300 / TS6200 |
 281: | 11 | merge a member into `World` via `declare module` | TS2300 / TS6200 |
 282: 
 283: ### The red-then-green demonstration â€” this is the deliverable, not a formality
 284: 
 285: `tests/at/typeprobes/tsconfig.json` includes `**/*.ts`, so both probe files compile together and a
 286: whole-program exit code cannot isolate the new attacks. The demonstration is therefore **per-file**:
 287: 
 288: 1. **RED (hole open).** At the pre-fix commit, compile `sut-seam.probe.ts` ALONE with the pinned
 289:    compiler and the probe config's options. Expected: **exit 0 â€” it compiles clean.** That is the
 290:    defect, executed rather than described.
 291: 2. **GREEN (hole shut).** At the fix commit, compile the same file the same way. Expected: non-zero,
 292:    with each attack's named diagnostic present.
 293: 
 294: Both transcripts are committed to `loop/items/AI4DEV-31/` verbatim. A claim of "it fails now" without
 295: the recorded "it passed before" is not evidence, and this item is about evidence.
 296: 
 297: The selftest must then assert each attack **by name**, in the style of the existing
 298: `ALIAS_PROTECTED` loop, so that removing any single protection fails a test that says which one.
 299: 
 300: ---
 301: 
 302: ## 4. No behaviour change â€” the bar, pinned to a measured baseline
 303: 
 304: Baseline taken on this worktree at `761aaa9` (origin/main), after `bun install`:
 305: 
 306: - `bun run typecheck` â†’ **exit 0**
 307: - `bun run at:verify req-016 --tier loop --expect` â†’ **exit 0**, `12 P0: 8 green, 4 red, 0 missing`,
 308:   matching `tests/at/expected/req-016.json` exactly.
 309: 
 310: Both must still hold, with the identical 8/4/0 split and the same four reds
 311: (AT-016.07, .09, .11 pending capabilities, plus the one shown above the fold). `bun run at:selftest`
 312: must also stay green.
 313: 
 314: ### A constraint the design must survive â€” found by reading, not by failing
 315: 
 316: `tests/at/harness/runner-blackbox.selftest.ts:63-69` and `runner-expect.selftest.ts:57-59` **generate
 317: synthetic suites at run time** into a disposable tree, whose shared preamble calls
 318: `bindSuite({ sut: 'probe', sutMissingDetail: â€¦ })` with no requirement and no type arguments. They
 319: run under requirements 901-906 and are never type-checked (they live outside every tsconfig), but
 320: they **are executed**, so D4's runtime cross-check will throw on them as written.
 321: 
 322: The fix is mechanical and must be done deliberately, not discovered: `suitePreamble()` takes the
 323: requirement and emits `bindSuite({ requirement: 'req-<n>', sut: 'probe', â€¦ })`, and every call site
 324: passes its own. Their assertions must not otherwise change â€” these tests are the runner's black-box
 325: guarantees and altering what they assert would trade this item's proof for theirs.
 326: 
 327: I considered making the cross-check fire only when a requirement is supplied, so these would pass
 328: untouched, and **rejected it**: a check that can be disabled by omitting a field is the hole again,
 329: arriving through the door marked "convenience".
 330: 
 331: ---
 332: 
 333: ## 5. Files in scope
 334: 
 335: | file | change |
 336: |---|---|
 337: | `tests/at/harness/suite-adapters.ts` | NEW â€” the derived registry and its type functions (types only) |
 338: | `tests/at/harness/registry.ts` | `bindSuite` / `atTest` derive rather than accept `Sut`/`W`; requirement cross-check; the cast at 544 narrowed and its comment rewritten to say what is now checked |
 339: | `tests/at/suites/req-016/_bind.ts` | names its requirement and sut key instead of two types |
 340: | `tests/at/suites/req-016/_contract.ts` | the seam-reachable `interface`s become type aliases (D5) |
 341: | `tests/at/typeprobes/sut-seam.probe.ts` | NEW â€” attacks 5-11 |
 342: | `tests/at/harness/type-invention.selftest.ts` | asserts each new attack by name |
 343: | `tests/at/harness/runner-blackbox.selftest.ts`, `runner-expect.selftest.ts` | generated preamble carries the requirement |
 344: | `loop/items/AI4DEV-31/` | design, gates, red-then-green transcripts, merge ruling |
 345: 
 346: Out of scope, explicitly: H3/H4/H5 capability work; any change to `createHarness`'s signature or to
 347: `AtHarness`; any change to what the runner selftests assert; `OpenWorld.h`.
 348: 
 349: ## 6. Open questions
 350: 
 351: None that I cannot decide. If Gate 1 finds the per-suite registry line (Â§2 cost) or the D5 scope
 352: ruling unacceptable, I rule on it; if a finding contradicts ratified text or demands scope this item
 353: does not own, it goes to the founder as a question, relayed verbatim through `main`.
`n--- tsconfigs ---
{
  /* ONE probe file, compiled alone, under the acceptance tree's real compiler settings.

     `files` rather than `include`, and `include: []` to cancel the inherited one, because the
     parent's `exclude` names `typeprobes` â€” `exclude` filters `include` and never filters `files`,
     so this is the only spelling that both inherits the settings and reaches the file.

     It extends the ACCEPTANCE config, not `typeprobes/tsconfig.json`: sharing a program with
     `harness-invention.probe.ts` would drag in that file's program-wide `declare module` blocks and
     its expected failures, and a shared exit code cannot isolate one attack from another.

     A config that matches no files also exits 0, so the transcripts in `loop/items/AI4DEV-31/`
     carry a `--listFiles` control proving the probe is actually in the program. */
  "extends": "../tsconfig.json",
  "files": ["sut-seam-legacy.probe.ts"],
  "include": []
}
{
  /* The NEW-API attacks, compiled alone. Same reasoning as `tsconfig.sut-seam-legacy.json`:
     `files` rather than `include` because the parent's `exclude` names `typeprobes` and `exclude`
     never filters `files`, and a private program rather than `typeprobes/tsconfig.json` because the
     other probe files carry program-wide `declare module` blocks whose diagnostics would be
     indistinguishable from these.

     Compiling each probe alone is what lets `type-invention.selftest.ts` assert a protection BY
     NAME and PER FILE. A combined exit code cannot: it is satisfied by any error anywhere, so a
     protection could be removed and the suite would stay green on the strength of its neighbours. */
  "extends": "../tsconfig.json",
  "files": ["sut-seam.probe.ts"],
  "include": []
}
{
  /* Every file under tests/at, whatever its extension - the point of this config is that a suite
     written next month is covered the day it is written. `allowJs`/`checkJs` are what make the
     coverage claim exactly true rather than nearly true: a JavaScript helper dropped in here runs
     under vitest, so it must be type-checked too, not merely discouraged by a README.
     The explicit `exclude` is load-bearing - naming one replaces TypeScript's default, and vitest
     recreates tests/at/node_modules (its own cache) on every at:selftest run. */
  "include": ["**/*"],
  /* `typeprobes` holds code that MUST NOT compile - the negative tests for the harness-invention
     attacks. A green run over that directory IS the defect, so it cannot live in this program; it
     has its own config and `type-invention.selftest.ts` asserts that it still fails. */
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "typeprobes"],
  "compilerOptions": {
    /* Node-side, NOT the browser. `target` matches the root config deliberately: vite resolves the
       nearest tsconfig per file, and this field drives useDefineForClassFields, so a divergence
       here would change class semantics in the harness at runtime. */
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "types": ["node"],

    /* The harness imports with explicit .ts specifiers ('./registry.ts'), which only Bundler
       resolution accepts; TypeScript requires noEmit alongside allowImportingTsExtensions. */
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,

    /* Checking, not linting. skipLibCheck stays FALSE: it cannot tell a third-party .d.ts from a
       first-party one under tests/at, and skipping our own declarations is what this item exists
       to stop. noUncheckedSideEffectImports matches the root config - without it a renamed bare
       side-effect import produces no diagnostic at all. */
    "skipLibCheck": false,
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true
  }
}



