   1: /**
   2:  * WHICH SUITES EXIST, AND WHAT EACH ONE'S ADAPTER REALLY PRODUCES â€” stated once, and checked.
   3:  *
   4:  * Before this file the harness knew its suites only as a path convention: `loadAdapter()` built
   5:  * `suites/req-0NN/_fixture.ts` from a string and imported it as `Partial<FixtureAdapterModule>`,
   6:  * whose `sut` is `Record<string, unknown>`. Every type the adapter's author had proved was thrown
   7:  * away at that boundary, and the consuming suite then RESTATED it â€” `bindSuite<NotificationsSut,
   8:  * World>` â€” as a claim nothing checked. Two independent statements about the same thing, with no
   9:  * mechanism able to notice them diverging: the exact failure shape this project's way of work
  10:  * exists to delete. `tests/at/typeprobes/sut-seam-legacy.probe.ts` is that hole, executed.
  11:  *
  12:  * So the suite stops naming its types. It names its requirement and its system-under-test key, and
  13:  * the types are READ OFF the adapter that actually supplies them, through `SutOf` / `WorldOf`
  14:  * below. There is one statement now, made by the producer, where it is already checked.
  15:  *
  16:  * ADDING A SUITE IS ONE LINE in `AdapterModules`, plus `export const requirement = 'req-0NN' as
  17:  * const;` in that suite's `_fixture.ts`. A suite whose line is missing cannot call `bindSuite` at
  18:  * all: the requirement literal is not a `SuiteId`, so the compiler says
  19:  * `Type '"req-999"' is not assignable to type '"req-016"'`. THAT NAMES THE PROBLEM AND NOT THE
  20:  * REMEDY â€” an earlier version of this comment claimed the error tells an author what to add, and it
  21:  * does not. tsc's messages are not ours to write, which is exactly why the two lines to add are
  22:  * spelled out in this paragraph instead. That ergonomic cost is deliberate: an explicit, checkable
  23:  * list of suites is worth more than a convention that silently resolves to whatever happens to be on
  24:  * disk.
  25:  *
  26:  * `typeof import(...)` sits in TYPE position only, so it is erased at emit and adds no runtime edge
  27:  * from the harness to the suites. It mirrors, at compile time, the resolution `index.ts` already
  28:  * performs at run time.
  29:  *
  30:  * WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE, said plainly because a closure claim wider than the truth is the very
  31:  * defect this file removes. The threat model here is A SUITE DRIFTING FROM THE HARNESS WITH NOBODY
  32:  * ABLE TO DETECT IT â€” an honest mistake that type-checks green. It is not an author determined to
  33:  * defeat the type system, and that author is still not stopped:
  34:  *
  35:  *   - `any`, `as`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-nocheck`
  36:  *   - mutating the adapter's object at run time
  37:  *   - pointing `AT_REPO_ROOT` at a different tree of `_fixture.ts` files
  38:  *   - STRUCTURAL RECONSTRUCTION: hand-building a widened context out of the DERIVED types â€”
  39:  *     `Omit<AtContext<R, K>, 'open'> & { open(): Promise<OpenWorld<R, K> & { sut: { â€¦ } }> }` â€” and
  40:  *     annotating a body with it. Measured compiling clean through every entry point, with no cast
  41:  *     and no suppression; see `SeamOpenWorld` in `registry.ts` for why no type can reject it and
  42:  *     `loop/items/AI4DEV-31/gate2-widen-reproduction.txt` for the transcript.
  43:  *
  44:  * No machinery is added for any of them, on purpose. Somebody who writes a cast â€” or who assembles
  45:  * a widened type out of `Omit` and an intersection â€” has made a decision; somebody whose suite
  46:  * quietly disagrees with its adapter has not, and only the second kind of failure is invisible.
  47:  */
  48: 
  49: import type { WorldLike } from './registry.ts';
  50: 
  51: /**
  52:  * The minimum any fixture adapter owes the harness, expressed so a malformed one fails AT ITS MAP
  53:  * ENTRY below rather than in whichever suite first opens a world.
  54:  *
  55:  * `fixtures.world` returning `Promise<WorldLike>` is the load-bearing part: `open()` pushes every
  56:  * world onto the teardown stack and calls `teardown()` on it, so an adapter whose world cannot be
  57:  * torn down would leak a frozen clock or an armed fault into the next AT id. That has to be a
  58:  * compile error where the adapter is registered, not a run-time surprise.
  59:  */
  60: type AdapterShape = {
  61:   sut: Record<string, unknown>;
  62:   fixtures: { world(name: string): Promise<WorldLike> };
  63:   teardown(): Promise<void>;
  64: };
  65: 
  66: /**
  67:  * What a fixture MODULE must export, parameterized by the key it is registered under.
  68:  *
  69:  * `requirement: R` is what ties the three facts that were previously independent: the key used for
  70:  * the TYPE lookup here, the module those types are read from, and the module `loadAdapter()`
  71:  * actually imports at run time. Without it `'req-016'` could name `req-017/_fixture.ts` by a typo
  72:  * and nothing would notice â€” the type-check would describe one suite while the run drove another.
